From 78b070ae90c3b6ae7976e8e504aa542922613ce5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 18:02:36 +0000
Subject: ipa: softisp: agc: Regulate onto a luminance target, with dynamic
 range compression

The AGC aims the mean sample value of a five bin histogram of the raw
image at the middle of its range, which puts the mean linear luminance
around 40-50% of full scale. Vendor AEC tunings aim much lower (a luma
target around a fifth of full scale on Qualcomm's), and a tone curve
taken from such a tuning is made for that exposure: combined with the
default target, anything bright in the scene comes out blown.

Add an optional meanTarget: the linear mean luminance above black, as a
fraction of full scale, to regulate onto instead. The correction is the
ratio in stops, taken straight (damped to 0.85) when more than 0.6 stop
off and half-way within the step limit when closer, with no correction
within 0.1 stop.

With a mean luminance target alone, a scene with bright areas in it is
exposed for its mean and the bright areas clip. Vendor AECs adjust the
target from the histogram and, rather than darkening the whole image to
save the highlights, expose lower and let the tone mapping lift the rest
back up (Qualcomm's ADRC: its metering tuning gives a ceiling for the
brightest few percent of the pixels and a largest gain, around 2.4 in
daylight).

Add an optional histogram block to the Agc tuning, after the parameters
of Qualcomm's histogram luma target (its implementation is not public):
the mean level of the brightest pixels in a range (0-5%), scaled back up
by the DRC gain in effect, is compared with a ceiling. Above it, exposure
is held below the target by their ratio, up to maxDrcGain and filtered,
and the target itself moves down by at most the lower bound of
targetAdj; below it, a flat scene, the target rises by up to the upper
bound. The DRC gain is kept in the active state, and Adjust applies it
at the start of the tone curve, on the linear input, as
x * g / (1 + (g - 1) * x): shadows and mid-tones are scaled back up by
the gain, white stays white.

The tone curve took a new DRC gain at once, while the exposure lowered
for it reaches the sensor frames later: the image brightened, then
darkened back as the exposure caught up, on every change. Worse, the
next statistics, of frames still taken at the old exposure, were scaled
by the new gain, so the loop overestimated the highlights and swung
back and forth.

Remember, for each exposure decision, its exposure x gain and the DRC
gain it was made for, and when a frame's statistics come in, look up the
DRC gain its actual exposure was decided for: that one is what the tone
curve uses and what the highlights are scaled by. Change the gain only
by 10% or more at a time, past the exposure loop's own dead band, so
that every gain comes with an exposure that tells it apart.

Clipped highlights read no brighter than full scale however bright they
are, which held the gain at about their ratio to the ceiling: keep
raising it by a quarter per update while they clip, up to the maximum.

The DRC tone curve changed in steps against the exposure, never in the
same frame: brightness pulsed on every step, as a staircase. With the
frame's actual exposure known when its parameters are computed, give
each frame the tone curve gain that brings its mean up to the target
without DRC, predicted from the last frame with statistics and the two
frames' exposure x gain, and at most the DRC gain its exposure was
decided for. The curve then follows the exposure exactly, whatever steps
the exposure loop takes, and the DRC gain no longer needs the dead band
that made each change a step of its own.

Name the number of luminance levels in SwIspStats and derive the full
scale and histogram bin width from it, instead of 255 and 256 literals.

Signed-off-by: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
